## Components of a fitted observation

For intercept $\alpha$ and slope $\beta _{j}$, the component attributed to factor j is its fitted exposure times the observed factor, not the factor's standalone variance.

**Level decomposition at observation t**

$$
\begin{gathered}
c_{j,t} = \beta _{j}\cdot f_{j,t} \\
y_{t} = \alpha  + \sum _{j} c_{j,t} + e_{t}
\end{gathered}
$$

The intercept must be retained when reconstructing levels. It is constant within a fitted window and contributes no within-window variance.

## Checks (compound put-call parity, exact)

$$
\begin{gathered}
\text{Call on call} - \text{Put on call} = \;\text{Call}\;(S, \mathrm{K2}, \mathrm{T2}) - \mathrm{K1} e^{-r \mathrm{T1}} \\
\text{Call on put}  - \text{Put on put}  = \;\text{Put}\;(S, \mathrm{K2}, \mathrm{T2})  - \mathrm{K1} e^{-r \mathrm{T1}}
\end{gathered}
$$

Call(S, K2, T2) / Put(S, K2, T2) are plain BSM prices with time T2. Also: every price >= 0, and as $\mathrm{T1} \to  \mathrm{T2}$ call on call
-> Call(S, K1 + K2, T2) and call on put -> Put(S, K2 - K1, T2) (when $\mathrm{K2} > \mathrm{K1}$).

